# Target C-II — angular-sector multiplicity sharpens the long-side contact floor

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** no-container full-core Type-II branch  
**Dependencies:** long-side straight-event dichotomy, loaded-route bridge suppression, audited `g=0.0021` low-`t` squeeze  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The current long-side bridge-suppression theorem loses a factor five when converting
near-straight outer-face occurrences to branching physical vertices.  That loss is not
necessary.  A `beta`-straight occurrence occupies a genuine outer-face angular sector
of width strictly larger than

\[
\pi-2\beta.
\]

For `beta<=pi/6`, three such sectors cannot fit around one physical vertex.  Therefore
a branching vertex supports at most two `beta`-straight outer-face occurrences, not
five.

After the existing edge-deduplication and bridge suppression, the factor `10` in the
transverse-contact theorem improves to `4`:

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {4E_\perp(\beta)\over D}
\ge
\liminf {L_{>2}\over D}
-\delta\left(1+{2\over1-\cos\beta}\right),
\qquad 0<\beta\le{\pi\over6}.
}
\tag{SM.1}
\]

At the audited `g=0.0021` low-`t` floor, taking `beta=pi/6` gives

\[
\boxed{
{E_\perp(\pi/6)\over D}
\ge0.0977831787370802\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{SM.2}

with every selected edge having transverse component strictly larger than `1/2`
relative to its associated long loaded-hull side direction.

This more than doubles the previous audited `0.0391D` floor without changing any
geometric premise.

## 1. Outer-face occurrences are angular sectors

Fix a physical contact vertex `x`.  In the straight-line plane embedding, every
occurrence of `x` on the outer-face boundary walk corresponds to one angular sector
between two consecutive incident contact rays in the cyclic rotation at `x`.
Distinct outer-face occurrences use distinct sectors: the face permutation assigns
one face occurrence to each such corner of the rotation system.

The angular widths of all sectors at `x` sum exactly to

\[
2\pi.
\tag{SM.3}
\]

This remains true when `x` is an articulation vertex and the outer face visits it more
than once.  Repeated boundary occurrences are precisely repeated visits through
different sectors; no simple-boundary assumption is used.

## 2. A straight occurrence consumes more than `pi-2 beta` of angle

Consider a `beta`-straight internal occurrence on one distinguished route `W_i`, with
predecessor `a`, vertex `x`, successor `b`, and loaded-side unit direction `u_i`.

By definition, the oriented route edge into `x` and the oriented route edge out of `x`
make angles of absolute value strictly below `beta` with `u_i`.  Hence the contact ray
from `x` to `a` lies within `beta` of `-u_i`, while the ray from `x` to `b` lies within
`beta` of `+u_i`.

Therefore the smaller angular separation of the two rays is strictly larger than

\[
\pi-2\beta.
\tag{SM.4}
\]

The complementary separation is at least `pi`, hence is also larger than
`pi-2 beta`.  The outer-face sector used by this occurrence is one of these two
separations, so its angular width is strictly larger than

\[
\boxed{\pi-2\beta.}
\tag{SM.5}

If

\[
0<\beta\le{\pi\over6},
\]

then every such sector has width strictly larger than `2pi/3`.  By (SM.3), a physical
vertex can support at most two `beta`-straight outer-face occurrences.

This is the only new geometric observation in the note.

## 3. Improved finite event tradeoff

Retain the notation of the long-side straight-event theorem:

- `S_beta` is the occurrence-counted number of `beta`-straight internal events on the
  long-side routes;
- `V_2(beta)` is the number of distinct degree-two physical vertices supporting such
  events;
- `B_beta` is the number of distinct degree-at-least-three physical vertices supporting
  such events;
- `E_perp(beta)` is the number of distinct selected transverse extra contacts, one
  chosen at each branching physical vertex and deduplicated as undirected edges.

The directional-energy estimate is unchanged:

\[
\boxed{
S_\beta
\ge
L_{>2}-{2d_{out}\over1-\cos\beta}.
}
\tag{SM.6}

A degree-two physical vertex supports at most two outer-face occurrences, so its
contribution remains at most `2V_2(beta)`.

By Section 2, a branching physical vertex also supports at most two `beta`-straight
occurrences.  Thus branching events contribute at most

\[
2B_\beta.
\tag{SM.7}
\]

One selected undirected extra contact edge can be selected at at most its two
endpoints, hence

\[
B_\beta\le2E_\perp(\beta).
\tag{SM.8}

Combining (SM.6)--(SM.8) gives the finite strengthened event inequality

\[
\boxed{
2V_2(\beta)+4E_\perp(\beta)
\ge
L_{>2}-{2d_{out}\over1-\cos\beta},
\qquad 0<\beta\le{\pi\over6}.
}
\tag{SM.9}

The previous theorem had coefficient `10` in front of `E_perp`.

Every edge counted by `E_perp(beta)` retains the existing geometric conclusion

\[
{|(w-x)\cdot Ju_i|}>\sin(\pi/3-\beta).
\tag{SM.10}

## 4. Insert the audited bridge suppression

The loaded internal-route theorem and bridge-subtracted isoperimetry give, along every
convergent full-core Type-II subsequence,

\[
\boxed{
\limsup {2V_2(\beta)\over D}\le\delta,
\qquad
\delta:=\lim {d_{out}\over D}.
}
\tag{SM.11}

Divide (SM.9) by `D`, pass to liminf/limsup, and use (SM.11).  This gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {4E_\perp(\beta)\over D}
\ge
\liminf {L_{>2}\over D}
-\delta\left(1+{2\over1-\cos\beta}\right).
}
\tag{SM.12}

This is (SM.1).

## 5. Audited `g=0.0021` endpoint

Use

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
C_0=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0021,
\]

and the current audited proof-only floor

\[
t_{21}={4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}\right)+0.0017279.
\]

The propagated scalar quantities are

\[
R(t_{21})=0.00567555,
\qquad
\delta\le0.002837775,
\tag{SM.13}
\]

and, on the rounded long-side branch,

\[
\boxed{
{L_{>2}\over D}
\ge
F_{21}-o(1),
\qquad
F_{21}=0.4363333718701161781\ldots .
}
\tag{SM.14}

Take

\[
\beta={\pi\over6}.
\]

Then

\[
1-\cos\beta=1-{\sqrt3\over2},
\qquad
\sin(\pi/3-\beta)={1\over2}.
\]

Equation (SM.12) yields

\[
\begin{aligned}
\liminf {E_\perp(\pi/6)\over D}
&\ge {1\over4}
\left[
F_{21}
-0.002837775
\left(1+{2\over1-\sqrt3/2}\right)
\right]\\
&=0.0977831787370802\ldots .
\end{aligned}
\tag{SM.15}

This proves (SM.2).

The corresponding transverse-flux floor is strictly larger than

\[
0.0488915893685401\ldots D-o(D),
\]

because every selected edge has transverse component greater than `1/2`.

## 6. Optional flux-oriented angle

For applications that use the product of edge count and transverse component rather
than raw count, one may choose any fixed `beta<pi/6` in (SM.12).  For example

\[
\beta={7\pi\over60}=21^\circ
\]

gives, at the same endpoint,

\[
{E_\perp(7\pi/60)\over D}
>0.0870-o(1),
\]

while every edge has transverse component greater than

\[
\sin(13\pi/60)=0.6293\ldots .
\]

The resulting product exceeds `0.0547D-o(D)`.  The exact theorem remains (SM.12); no
numerical optimizer is used as a proof premise.

## 7. Scope audit

- The improvement concerns multiplicity of **outer-face occurrences at one physical
  vertex**.  It does not identify repeated physical vertices or discard articulation
  multiplicity.
- Distinct outer-face occurrences correspond to distinct rotation sectors even at a
  cutvertex.
- Strict `< beta` in the straight-event definition makes the sector width strict in
  (SM.5), so `beta=pi/6` still permits at most two events.
- Edge deduplication still loses a factor two because one physical extra contact can be
  selected at both endpoints; no unsupported injectivity is claimed.
- Bridge suppression is imported exactly in its audited asymptotic form.
- All selected objects are actual unit-contact edges; no KKT coefficient floor is used.
- The numerical endpoint uses the independently audited `g=0.0021`, not the newer
  provisional `0.002123` value.
- No exact unit equilateral triangle or strict same-cardinality sub-`D` competitor is
  produced.

**Disposition:** proof-only strengthening.  C-II and Level I remain open.
